the pleasure to welcome  of our students here for the ZL family  weekend on all of you whatever your  occasion for visiting we have a number  of old friends visiting I saw Stefan and  I see yang and showers back and that's  good to see feel our community coming  back together to the parents I want to  say thank you for loaning us your  children for these years I hope it's a  good experience for them and I know it  will be for us as I say each year in  clarification for the parents when  you're leaving the church today and  you're shaking my hand the fact that I  do not recognize your child or know  their name has absolutely nothing to do  with their pattern of attendance at  church it is clearly the fault of my  aging brain alone every year I have to  say we seem to be cursed with the worst  possible scripture texts for parents we  get I think this is by far the worst  we've had though last year i believe we  had Jesus saying whoever comes to me and  does not hate their mother and their  father cannot be my disciple this year  is not much better we come to patel  chapel in hopes of a word of gentle  inspiration of unity and hope and what  do we get all four of our scriptures or  about how the rich are going to help a  little light reading for sunday from 1st  timothy we get the well-worn maxim for  the love of money is the root of all  kinds of evil as ron read from the  Prophet Amos we get the warning to those  who are comfortable on there I be  couches who feel secure who enjoy the  good life that they will be the first in  Israel to be exiled even the psalmist  who we can usually trust to be kind to  us on rough lectionary days reminds us  that our hope and happiness yes is in  God but that this god is first and  foremost about justice for the oppressed  and food for the hungry and finally this  brutal story Ted read from Luke's Gospel  if you know it you know how tough it is  with the rich man  the one who feasted sumptuously in  purple robes now roasting in Hades while  the poor man ignored by the rich man  though he was dumped on the rich man  steps every day is now in heaven sitting  on father Abraham's lap and not only  that the justice of this reversal of  fortunes isn't even open for discussion  father Abraham says sorry that's the way  it is the rich man had a good in this  life and so now is in agony and the poor  man suffered evil and so is comforted  this is the good news of the gospel  especially at Yale these are sort of  hard words to hear we are a very very  privileged community and I don't know if  your upbringing was anything like mine  but polite people don't talk about money  my mother always said talking about  money was goes and was not for polite  conversation here we have very many rich  people in our community and also very  many poor people so talking about money  risks high emotion and sometimes  defensiveness talking about money makes  everyone uncomfortable everyone worries  that they're judged because they have  too much or too little and so we just  don't talk about it except in the most  clinical and distant economic terms but  sadly the Bible this Sunday won't let us  alone well there's no easy way to  spiritualize this message the prophets  keep poking at everything we want to  keep secret the prophets keep sticking a  fork into that point that we most want  to hide I think the reality is that our  idea of money in this society is as  twisted as it was for the rich man a few  generations back it seems like faith was  our foundation our source and money was  a necessary evil to be carefully kept in  its place but today i would suggest  money and security are now  are unquestioned religion while  Christianity today I think is seen as a  potentially dangerous option to be  carefully fenced in and not allowed to  affect too much of what's really  important to life I talked to a lot of  students here as you would imagine who  have big questions about Christianity  and many of whom are very skeptical and  they say to me how could you accept such  a dogmatic faith that flies in the face  of science and reason and demand such  utter devotion and I think to myself  which faith are we talking about is it  God or is it money a few generations ago  I think religion was just assumed all  right-thinking people believed it and  only a few odd characters questioned it  but today I think a certain kind of  free-market economics is our  unquestioned Dogma unquestioned and  unquestionable even if it flies in the  face of our historical experience  especially the last five years I think  money is taking over more and more of  our lives and it demands complete  devotion and blind faith and we give it  excuse me our scripture writers weren't  critical of wealth because they were  socialists are anarchists they were not  communists wealth they said could be a  blessing from God but instead they saw  in their own times that the love of  money caused bad things to happen caused  inequality and suffering and corruption  love of money cause people to forget  their covenant responsibilities to their  neighbors love of money perverted  religion in the state and even business  sounds strangely familiar and it would  be wonderful have this applied only to  the one percent at the top or the one  tenth of one percent or the one  hundredth of one percent but those folks  couldn't get away with it they couldn't  get away with what they get away with if  we didn't buy into the same dog mother  put them where they are I don't know  about you but I think about money I  think about the future and I  hurry I worry about retirement savings  and home repairs and all sorts of silly  things and I spent a lot of time  thinking about it and what struck me as  I was writing the sermon I was I was  thinking about that rich man and that  poor man is that God never comes into  that conversation in my head money is in  its own little territory over here and  it has nothing to do with God it is its  own God I think we've been persuaded  that economics is real and God as a  pleasant fantasy we've been convinced  that it's impolite to say that love of  wealth could be immoral or even unholy  this summer I was on vacation and I  heard professor michael sandel who  teaches at some College in Cambridge  Massachusetts I've never heard of it and  he argued that the free market  revolution of the last 35 years means  that now everything is available for a  price never thought about this way but  he gave examples he said in Santa Ana  California you can upgrade your jail  cell for 82 dollars a night he said you  can now pay a woman in india six  thousand two hundred and fifty dollars  to carry our child as a surrogate mother  you can pay ten thousand dollars for  designer eggs and sperm to guarantee a  perfect child and most scarce of all you  can pay fifteen hundred dollars a year  and get your doctor's cell phone number  lobbyists in washington are paying  homeless people fifteen to twenty  dollars an hour to hold places in line  for them at important hearings send out  questions whether it's right for markets  to run more and more of our lives for  there to be for-profit schools and  hospitals and prisons and police and now  even a for-profit military and is  grounds for questioning are not  themselves religious but they sound just  like Amos and Timothy in a society where  everything up is up for sale inequality  grows and the poor suffer more deeply  then send l has this passage he says if  the only advantage of affluence was the  ability to buy yachts or sports cars or  fancy vacations  inequality wouldn't matter much but as  money comes to buy more and more  political influence good medical care a  home in a safe neighborhood access to  elite schools the distribution of income  and wealth looms larger and larger and  we're all good things are bought and  sold having lots of money makes all the  difference in the world it can separate  life and death sindel also argues that  putting every public good up for sale  corrupts these goods I never thought of  it this way he says markets not only  allocate goods neutrally but they  express and promote certain values and  that perhaps some things should not be  put up for sale education he said health  perhaps there are things that shouldn't  be for sale historically we've decided  that people should not be put up for  sale we called that slavery children  should not be bought or sold you should  be able to pay someone to do your jury  duty for you it's a funny thing that we  draw the line there that you can't sell  your vote on eBay we do draw lines I  think we're getting perilously close to  breaking these ancient rules without  even realizing it I think we've handed  over our society to the religion of  money to the free market which proudly  says it is a moral in st. Luke story  about the rich man and Lazarus the rich  man's problem was not his money it was  that he never saw Lazarus lying at his  doorstep Lazarus was there every day  begging for crumbs but the rich man was  surrounded by high walls and many  servants whose job it was to be sure  that he never saw anything unpleasant  well doesn't have to insulate us from  the suffering of the world but most  often it does as John Stendahl has  written the chasm between Hades and  Abraham's abode is wide and unbridgeable  in the scripture but no wider or more  unbridgeable than the chasm between the  rich  man's feasting room and Lazarus laying  in his steps and we are living in a  world where the chasm between rich and  poor is becoming just as wide and just  as unbridgeable more than ever the rich  are getting richer and the poor are  locked into poverty without hope and my  message today from our scripture is that  God cares about this I read on Friday  about Robert benmashi chairman of AIG  one of the Corporations responsible for  the 2008 financial collapse who recently  compared public opposition to high  executive bonuses to lynchings in the  Jim Crow South really it would be easy  to hate the rich but then I think that  compared to most of the world I'm the  rich be easy to blame the rich for  everything but the problem I think is  much bigger than that it isn't just a  few folks we've taken God and morality  out of the world and replaced them with  an amoral mechanism and unquestioning  faith that the free flow of capital is a  natural law and supersedes God's law  sandell writes markets don't pass  judgments they don't have morality if  someone is willing to pay for sex or a  kidney and a consenting adult is willing  to sell the only question the market  asks is how much but we do come to  church for good news not bad news right  and there is good news today God is  bigger than the Federal Reserve the IMF  the New York Stock Exchange and the  fortune 500 and God's prophets want to  tell us that we don't have to live in  this kind of society we're enthralled by  markets and money under a kind of spell  that says justice and morality really  don't exist and even if they did there'd  be no way to enforce them but our  scriptures break the spell they remind  us that our communities are bound by a  covenant to care for the poor in the  suffering too  recognized injustice and corruption  around us and to believe that God and  God's people have the power to change  anything even our economic system I  wonder what would happen if instead of  presuming that we have to start with  this system what if we were to presume  to start with God's kingdom where  justice rolls down like waters and  righteousness like an ever-flowing  stream if that kingdom were our starting  point and we just worked out the details  from there Jesus had a lot to say about  wealth and poverty as he as does the  whole Bible but most of all Jesus  himself wanted to bridge the chasm that  isolates the poor and gives give a new  vision where dishonest tax collectors  would volunteer to give back their money  that's one Bible story where rich young  ruler give all they have to the poor  where God reaches out to the poor and  demands of the church and the state do  justice for them Jesus he's the poor not  as a social problem to be solved but as  individuals beloved by God each with a  story each with a heart each sought by  God like the Shepherd for the lost sheep  what we're going to do here today in  this church I think opens a door will  come to this table in a few moments  where everyone eats the same bread and  drinks the same wine where there is no  first class or there is no platinum  reward level and after worship will make  sandwiches not fancy sandwiches just  plain sandwiches ham and turkey and  peanut butter and jelly and we'll go out  on the green after church and we'll do  our best to treat every person we meet  there as an honored guest on this day I  want to think about the possibility that  money is just a tool money is just  something we can use for good ends or  bad ends and that we are not locked into  a system  from which we cannot escape that there  is such a thing as holiness and  righteousness and morality and our use  of money and I don't want to claim I've  got it because I know I don't you don't  want to look at my credit card bill I  spend money on really stupid things a  lot of it I spent five hundred dollars  on a pair of shoes over summer vacation  and I love those shoes  but I think there is a way for us to  reclaim our lives from this system that  entraps us and the system that makes us  unhappy and uncomfortable because we see  the state of the world we're in and we  want to break free and the gospel shows  us the way shows us a different way a  different vision so I want to thank you  parents and families for sending your  children here they are remarkable they  are super smart they are hopeful and  they are motivated and I think the final  good news this Sunday is that even with  this word from these scary texts the  good news is that we are passing along  to these students a prophetic vision  where justice and equality and hope our  god-given and God guaranteed for all  people the good news is that they will  see the world differently and they will  challenge what we presumed was the only  way to run the world and they will show  us new paths to God's kingdom oh man

